* {{jump|s|is|horse}} {{l|is|hross}}, {{qualifier|poetic}} {{l|is|drösull}}, {{qualifier|archaic|poetic}} {{l|is|drasill}}

====Usage notes====
* The noun {{term|hestur|lang=is}} is the ''de facto'' noun used to determine the case of another noun by substitution. 
*: Using the noun {{term|maður||a man|lang=is}} as an example:
*:: {{term||ég þekki '''mann'''|I know a '''man'''}} → {{term||ég þekki '''hest'''|I know a '''horse'''}}
*: since {{term|hest|lang=is}} is known to be the accusative form of {{term|hestur|lang=is}} it may be deduced that {{term|mann|lang=is}} is in the accusative case as well.
* A commonly taught [[mnemonic]] to find the cases of nouns is reciting a set of prepositions followed by an agreeing noun and {{term|hestur|lang=is}} is often used as an example:
*: ''[[nominative case|nominative]]'': {{term||hér er '''hestur'''|there is a '''horse''' here}}
*: ''[[accusative case|accusative]]'': {{term||um '''hest'''|about a '''horse'''}}
*: ''[[dative case|dative]]'': {{term||frá '''hesti'''|from a '''horse'''}}
*: ''[[genitive case|genitive]]'': {{term||til '''hests'''|to a '''horse'''}}

